//===========================================================================
//First chapter:  Introduction
//===========================================================================
BEGIN CHAPTER 0

NOTATION ALGEBRAIC

"This tutorial explains the basic rules and moves of chess.  If you already 
know chess basics, see Chess Tutor to learn about play strategies."
OK

"Press the 'Spacebar' to move ahead  after you read a screen.  Try it now."
OK

"If you are using a mouse, you may also click on the 'OK' button to move on 
to the next screen."
OK

"Click on the 'Cancel' button or press the 'Esc' key to exit the tutorial."
OK

"You play chess on an 8 x 8 board of light and dark squares."
OK

"Always turn the board so that a light square is in the lower right corner."
OK

"<Light on Right> is an easy way to remember this."
OK

"Rows on the chessboard are called RANKS (1 to 8).  
These Pawns are all on rank 4."
BOARD 32/PPPPPPPP WHITE
OK

"Columns on the chessboard are called FILES (a to h).  These Pawns are all 
on the d-file."
BOARD 3P4/3P4/3P4/3P4/3P4/3P4/3P4/3P4 WHITE
OK

"Hint:  RANK and ROW both start with R.  This is an easy way to remember 
rank and file."
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

"Chess is a war between two opposing armies:  White and Black."
OK

"Each army has a King, a Queen, two Rooks, two Bishops, two Knights, and 
eight Pawns."
OK

"Start by setting-up the White army on your side of the board."
OK

"Put one Rook at each end of rank 1.  The Rook looks like a castle or tower."
OK

"The rook's castle shape comes from the Farsi Indian pieces which represented 
the tower carried by the elephant."
BOARD 56/R6R WHITE
OK

"Next to each Rook, place a Knight.  The Knights look like horses."
OK

"The Knight was originally called faras in Arabic, meaning horse.  In Europe, 
the name of the horse evolved to the name of it's rider, the Knight."
BOARD 56/RN4NR WHITE
OK

"Add a Bishop next to each Knight.  The Bishop has a notch in his hat."
OK

"There are two empty squares left on the near rank.  The Queen is placed on 
the square that matches its color."
BOARD 56/RNB2BNR WHITE
OK

"'Queen on Color' is an easy way to remember where the Queen goes.  (White 
Queen on a White square).  Or... 'The dress matches the shoes.' "
BOARD 56/RNBQ1BNR WHITE
OK

"The King goes on the last empty square."
OK

"At the front of the army go the Pawns, or foot soldiers.  Put one Pawn 
directly in front of each piece."
BOARD 56/RNBQKBNR WHITE
OK

"Now turn the board around and set up Black's pieces.  Black's pieces always 
start on rank 8."
BOARD 48/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
OK

"Two Black Rooks..."
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/48 BLACK
OK

"Two Black Knights..."
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/40/r6r BLACK
OK

"Two Black Bishops..."
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/40/rn4nr BLACK
OK

"Queen on Color!  (Black Queen on Black Square)"
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/40/rnb2bnr BLACK
OK

"The Black King"
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/40/rnbq1bnr BLACK
OK
          
"Pawns in front of pieces."
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/40/rnbqkbnr BLACK
OK

"White always makes the first move in a game.  Rotate the board so that 
White's pieces are in front."
BOARD RNBQKBNR/PPPPPPPP/32/pppppppp/rnbqkbnr BLACK
OK

"Now you're ready to play Chess!"
BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
OK

"Starting with White, each side takes turns moving."
MOVELIST "e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6"
OK

"You are allowed to move only ONE piece on each turn."
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

END CHAPTER
