BEGIN CHAPTER 1

"There are 6 different chess pieces..."
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

"Kings,"
BOARD 4k3/48/4K3 WHITE
OK

"Queens,"
BOARD 3q3/48/3Q4 WHITE
OK

"Rooks,"
BOARD r6r/48/R6R WHITE
OK
 
"Knights,"
BOARD 1n4n1/48/1N4N1 WHITE
OK
 
"Bishops,"
BOARD 2b2b2/48/2B2B2 WHITE
OK
 
"and Pawns."
BOARD 8/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/8 WHITE
OK

"Each piece has its own way of moving on the chessboard."
OK

"To play chess, you must learn how each piece moves."
BOARD 64 WHITE
OK

END CHAPTER
//==========================================================================
//"The King"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 2

//"The King"
BOARD 4k3/48/4K3 WHITE
"The King is the most important chess piece." 
OK
 
"If you CHECKMATE your opponent's King, you WIN!!" 
OK

"If your King is checkmated, you lose!"
OK

"In a checkmate, the King is not captured.  Instead, you win if you could 
capture your opponent's King on your next move and there is no move your 
opponent can make to prevent that from happening."
OK

"After seeing how the other pieces move, you will return to the subject of 
checkmate."
OK

"First, look at how the King moves."
OK

"The King can move one square in any direction.  (The ghost Kings show the 
squares to which this White King can legally move.)"
BOARD 40/3KKK2/3KKK2/3KKK2 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING e3
GHOST WHITE KING f3
GHOST WHITE KING f2
GHOST WHITE KING f1
GHOST WHITE KING e1
GHOST WHITE KING d1
GHOST WHITE KING d2
GHOST WHITE KING d3
OK

"Important Rule #1:  You may never move one of your pieces (even the King) 
to a square occupied by another of your pieces."
OK

"For example, the White King on e2 can move to every square EXCEPT f2, which 
is occupied by another White piece (a Pawn)."
BOARD 40/3KKK2/3KKP2/3KKK2 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING e3
GHOST WHITE KING f3
GHOST WHITE KING f1
GHOST WHITE KING e1
GHOST WHITE KING d1
GHOST WHITE KING d2
GHOST WHITE KING d3
OK

"Important Rule #2:  If an OPPOSING piece occupies a square to which you can 
move, you can CAPTURE the opposing piece by moving to that square, and 
removing your opponent's piece from the board."
OK

"For example, the White King can capture the Black Pawn on e3 because the 
White King can move to that square."
BOARD 40/3KpK2/3KKP2/3KKK2 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING f3
GHOST WHITE KING f1
GHOST WHITE KING e1
GHOST WHITE KING d1
GHOST WHITE KING d2
GHOST WHITE KING d3
OK

"King takes (captures) Pawn."
BOARD 40/4p3/4KP2/8 WHITE
MOVE Ke3
OK

"Although the King is the most important piece, it is also one of the weakest, 
because it can't move very far in a single turn."
BOARD 32/3KKK2/3KKK2/3KKK2/8 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING e4
GHOST WHITE KING f4
GHOST WHITE KING f3
GHOST WHITE KING f2
GHOST WHITE KING e2
GHOST WHITE KING d2
GHOST WHITE KING d3
GHOST WHITE KING d4
OK

"To win the game, the King must rely on the rest of his army."
OK

END CHAPTER
