GHOST WHITE BISHOP e3
GHOST WHITE BISHOP f2
GHOST WHITE BISHOP g1
"The Bishop moves along light or dark diagonals."
OK

BOARD 16/2N1N3/1N3N2/3N4/1N3N2/2N1N3/8 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT c6
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT e6
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT c2
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT e2
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT f5
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT f3
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT b5
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT b3
"The Knight makes L-shaped moves.  Two squares along a rank or file, then 
one square at a right angle."
OK

BOARD 24/3P4/3P4/24 WHITE
GHOST WHITE PAWN d5
"Pawns normally move one square towards the opposite side of the board, 
except when capturing."
OK

BOARD 24/2P1P3/3P4/24 WHITE
NOGHOST d5
GHOST BLACK PAWN c5
GHOST BLACK PAWN e5
"Pawns move forward, but capture DIAGONALLY!  All other pieces capture the 
same way they move."
OK

BOARD 32/4P3/4P3/4P3/8 WHITE
GHOST WHITE PAWN e3
GHOST WHITE PAWN e4
"The FIRST time it moves, a Pawn may travel one or two squares forward."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"Except for special moves (castling, promotion, and en passant captures), 
that's all there is to know about moving the various chess pieces."
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Check and Checkmate"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 9

//"Check and Checkmate"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

BOARD 1n2k2/16/7r/8/4K3/8/5Q2 WHITE
"If it's your turn to move, and your King could be captured on your 
opponent's next move, you are IN CHECK."
OK

MOVE Qb5
"Check"
OK

"Here, the White Queen threatens to capture the Black King, so Black is in 
check."
OK

"If your King is in check, your very next move MUST remove the threat."
OK

"There are 3 ways to remove a threat to your King."
OK

"1) Move your King to a square that is not under attack by your opponent."
OK

SLIDE e8 f8
"Or..."
OK

SLIDE f8 e8
"2) Capture the threatening piece with one of your pieces."
OK

SLIDE h5 b5
"Or..."
OK

SLIDE b5 h5
BOARD 1n2k3/16/1Q5r/8/4K3/16 BLACK
"3) Block the threat by moving one of your other pieces."
OK

MOVE Nd7
"If you cannot move, capture, or block, you are CHECKMATED and you lose the 
game."
OK

"Here is a very simple example (known as the Fool's Mate)."
BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
MOVELIST "f3 e5 g4 Qh4"
OK

"Checkmate, Black wins!"
OK

"White cannot move the King out of check, capture Black's Queen, or block the 
Black Queen's attack on e1, so White is checkmated and Black wins."
OK

"The object of the game is to checkmate your opponent, while avoiding 
checkmate yourself."
OK

BOARD 4k3/40/7r/4K3 WHITE
"By the way, you are not allowed to move INTO check.  In other words, you 
cannot make a move that lets your opponent capture your King."
OK

"For example, the White King cannot move to e2, d2, or f2."
OK

"All those moves would let Black's Rook capture the White King immediately.  
White would be giving up by making such a move."
OK

"If you are certain that you cannot win, you may RESIGN rather than wait for 
your opponent to checkmate you.  This says to your opponent, OK, you win!"
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Stalemate"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 10

//"Stalemate"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"A STALEMATE occurs when the side to move is not in check AND all possible 
moves place that side in check."
OK

"Since it is illegal to move into check, the side to move cannot move.  BUT, 
the side to move is not in check, so it's not checkmate."
OK

"The result:  STALEMATE, which is considered to be a draw (neither side 
wins)."
OK

BOARD 8/6R1/24/5K2/7k/8 WHITE
"Here's an example.  (Assume it is White's turn to move.)"
OK

"White starts by making a bad move."
OK

MOVE Rg2
"Check"
OK

"Black is in check, so must move out of the way."
OK

MOVE Kh1
"White now makes another bad move."
OK

MOVE Kf2
"It is now Black's turn.  The only possible moves (King to h2 or g1) would 
put the King in check, which is illegal."
OK

"Since Black cannot move, but is not in check, it is a Stalemate."
OK

"Black was lucky.  White SHOULD have won this game with King and Rook against 
King."
OK

BOARD 8/6R1/24/5K2/7k/8 WHITE
"Here's how:"
OK

"White starts by moving..."
OK

MOVE Rg3
"Black's only legal move is..."
OK

MOVE Kh1
"White responds with..."
OK

MOVE Rh3
"Check!"
OK

"Again, Black has only one legal move..."
OK

MOVE Kg1
"Here's the tricky part:  White needs to waste one move so that Black will be 
forced into a desired position (this is called temporizing)."
OK

"White temporizes with..."
OK

MOVE Rh4
"Black is forced to move..."
OK

MOVE Kf1
"And now White wins with..."
OK

MOVE Rh1
"Checkmate!"
OK

"A good chess player must understand checkmate AND stalemate.  In the 
previous example, Black avoided a certain loss because White did not see 
the possibility of a stalemate."
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"En Passant"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 11
 
BOARD 64 WHITE
//"Special Moves:  En Passant"
//OK

"En Passant is a special type of capture move for Pawns ONLY."
OK

BOARD 8/5p2/16/4P3/24 WHITE
"Suppose you just moved your White Pawn to e5"
OK

MOVE e5
"and your opponent moves a Pawn two squares forward to f5, landing next to 
you."
OK

MOVE f5
"In this case, you can capture the Black Pawn EN PASSANT (in passing), 
by moving your Pawn diagonally behind Black's pawn on your very next move."
OK

BOARD 16/5P2/40 WHITE
"Pawn takes Pawn, en passant"
OK

"You may capture en passant ONLY when your opponent moved a Pawn two squares 
forward on the previous move."
OK

"Here is an example of Black capturing White's Pawn en passant."
BOARD 32/1p6/8/P7/8 WHITE
OK

MOVE a4
"White just moved a Pawn two squares forward."
OK

"And the Black Pawn captures on the very next move by moving diagonally 
behind the White Pawn."
BOARD 40/p7/16 WHITE
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"This is a useful move when your opponent tries to sneak a Pawn past yours 
by moving two squares."
OK

"But remember:  If you wish to capture en passant, you must do so on your 
very next move."
OK

END CHAPTER
 
//==========================================================================
//"Castling"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 12

//"Special Moves:  Castling"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK
 
"Castling is a special move involving the King and either Rook."
BOARD 56/R3K2R WHITE
OK

BOARD 56/R1K1K1RR WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING g1
GHOST WHITE KING c1
"Castling moves two pieces.  First, move the King two squares toward the Rook."
OK

BOARD 56/R3K1RR WHITE
SLIDE e1 g1
"Then put the Rook on the other side of the King."
OK

SLIDE h1 f1
"You can castle with either Rook."
OK

BOARD 56/R1K1K1KR WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING g1
GHOST WHITE KING c1
"Here the King castles with the other Rook"
OK

BOARD 56/R3K2R WHITE
MOVE O-O-O
"If the King moves toward the nearer Rook (on the King's side of the board),"
OK

BOARD 56/R3K2R WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING g1
MOVE O-O
"it is called CASTLING KINGSIDE (written O-O)."
OK

BOARD 56/R1K1K2R WHITE
GHOST WHITE KING c1
"If the King moves toward the other Rook (on the Queen's side of the board),"
OK

BOARD 56/R3K2R WHITE
MOVE O-O-O
"it is called CASTLING QUEENSIDE (written O-O-O)."
OK

BOARD 56/R3K2R WHITE
"To be legal, the King and Rook must not have moved from their starting 
squares,"
OK

"the squares between the King and Rook must be unoccupied,"
OK

"the King cannot be in check"
OK

"and the square that the King passes over cannot be under attack.  
Of course, the King can never be moved INTO check, so the destination 
can't be under attack either."
OK

"It is usually a good idea to castle early in the game, as it protects the 
King and gives the Rook a chance to control the center files."
OK

END CHAPTER
 
//==========================================================================
//"Promotion"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 13

//"Special Moves:  Promotion"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

BOARD 1k6/p7/32/4P3/K7 WHITE
"When a Pawn reaches the opposite side of the Board,"
OK

MOVE e4 
MOVE a5 
MOVE e5 
MOVE Kb7 
MOVE e6 
MOVE Kb6 
MOVE e7 
MOVE a4
MOVE e8Q
"it must be PROMOTED to a Queen, Rook, Bishop, or Knight."
OK

"Normally, you choose to promote your Pawn to a Queen."
OK

BOARD 6R1/p4P1k/4B2p/32/4K3 WHITE
"Promoting a pawn to a piece other than a Queen is called UNDERPROMOTION."
OK

"Here, White underpromotes a Pawn to a Knight to achieve checkmate."
OK

"Checkmate."
MOVE f8N
OK

BOARD 4k3/24/p7/16/4K3 BLACK
"Because of promotion, an opponent's Pawn that is unopposed is a dangerous 
threat."
OK

"Here, the White King is too far away to capture the Black Pawn."
MOVELIST "a3 Kd1 a2 Kc1 a1Q"
OK

"Black can now win the game with ease."
OK

"Remember:  Only Pawns can be promoted.  This makes Pawns more valuable 
than they seem at first glance.  A promotion can instantly change the 
balance of power in a game."
OK

"By the way:  You may promote a Pawn to a Queen even if you already have 
one (or more) on the board.  It is possible (but extremely unlikely) to 
promote all eight of your Pawns to other pieces."
BOARD 64 WHITE 
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Algebraic Notation"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 14

//"Algebraic Notation"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"Algebraic Notation is the generally accepted method of writing down the 
moves in a chess game."
OK

"It is easy to learn and will increase your enjoyment of chess."
OK

"To describe a move in algebraic notation, start by writing down the letter 
of the piece that is moving."
OK

BOARD 40/4K3/16 WHITE
"K for a King"
OK

BOARD 40/4Q3/16 WHITE
"Q for a Queen"
OK

BOARD 40/4R3/16 WHITE     
"R for a Rook"
OK

BOARD 40/4B3/16 WHITE     
"B for a Bishop"
OK

BOARD 40/4N3/16 WHITE      
"N for a Knight"
OK

BOARD 40/4P3/16 WHITE     
"If a Pawn is moving, don't write down anything."
OK

"Then, write down the file (a - h) and rank (1 - 8) of that piece's 
destination square."
OK

"See how this might work for the first few moves of a real game."
BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
OK

"White opens by moving the Pawn to square e4.  So you write:"
OK

MOVE e4
"e4"
OK

"Black responds by moving the Pawn to square e5.  So you write:"
OK

MOVE e5
"e5"
OK

"White moves the Knight to square f3.  So you write:"
OK

MOVE Nf3
"Nf3"
OK

"Then Black moves the Knight to square c6.  So you write:"
OK

MOVE Nc6
"Nc6"
OK

"White attacks by moving the Bishop to b5.  So you write:"
OK

MOVE Bb5
"Bb5"
OK

"Black threatens that Bishop by moving the Pawn from a7 to a6:"
OK

MOVE a6
"a6"
OK

"and White moves the Bishop out of harm's way:"
OK

MOVE Ba4
"Ba4"
OK

"Black takes this opportunity to develop the other Knight:"
OK

MOVE Nf6
"Nf6"
OK

"Algebraic notation uses 'x' to indicate a capture."
OK

"For example, if you use the White Bishop to capture the Black Knight on 
square c6, you write:"
OK

"Bxc6"
MOVE Bc6
OK

"When a Pawn makes a capture, you also write down the file on which it 
starts:"
OK

"dxc6 (the Pawn started on the d-file)"
MOVE dxc6
OK

"Going back to the game, White moves the d-Pawn one square forward:"
OK

"d3"
MOVE d3
OK

"and Black responds by checking with the Bishop:"
OK

MOVE Bb4
"Check."
OK

"When a move places the other side in check, put a '+' at the end.  So you 
write this move as:"
OK

"Bb4+"
OK

"Sometimes, identical pieces can move to the same square."
OK

BOARD r1bqk2r/1pp2ppp/p1p2n2/4p3/1b2P3/3P1N2/PPPN1PPP/RNBQK2R WHITE
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT d2
"For example, White can remove the threat to the King by moving a Knight 
to d2."
OK

"Unfortunately, Nd2 doesn't tell you which White Knight moved.  
(Was it the Knight on f3 or the Knight on b1?)"
OK

"When identical pieces can move to the same square, algebraic notation does 
the following:"
OK

"If both pieces start on the SAME file, put the starting rank (1 - 8) right 
after the name of the piece."
OK

"Otherwise, put the starting file (a - h) right after the name of the piece."
OK

"In this case, the two White Knights are on DIFFERENT files.  So you write:"
OK

BOARD r1bqk2r/1pp2ppp/p1p2n2/4p3/1b2P3/3P1N2/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R WHITE
MOVE Nfd2
"Nfd2 (starting file after the piece name)"
OK

"Castling Kingside is written O-O.  Castling Queenside is written as O-O-O:"
OK

MOVE O-O
"O-O (Black castles Kingside)"
OK

MOVE O-O
"O-O (White castles Kingside)"
OK

BOARD 2k5/4P3/R7/32/4K2R WHITE
"When a promotion occurs, write the letter of the new piece at the end of 
the move:"
OK

MOVE e8Q
"e8Q+ (the new Queen checks the Black King)"
OK

MOVE Kc7
"Kc7"
OK

"When a move causes checkmate, write an extra '+' at the end of it:"
OK

MOVE Rh7
"Rh7++"
OK

"Now you know how to read and understand chess games recorded with Algebraic 
Notation.  To learn more about chess strategies, check out the Chess Tutor."
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Long Algebraic Notation"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 15

//"Long Algebraic Notation"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"Long algebraic notation is based on normal algebraic, which is described in 
the preceding tutorial chapter."
OK

"In long algebraic notation, the starting square is written for each move 
along with the destination square."
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
"Here's a sample game using long algebraic notation:"
OK

MOVE e4
"e2-e4"
OK

MOVE e5
"e7-e5"
OK

MOVE Nf3
"Ng1-f3"
OK

MOVE Nc6
"Nb8-c6"
OK

MOVE Bb5
"Bf1-b5"
OK

MOVE a6
"a7-a6"
OK

MOVE Ba4
"Bb5-a4"
OK

MOVE Nf6
"Ng8-f6"
OK

MOVE Bc6
"Ba4xc6"
OK

MOVE dxc6
"d7xc6"
OK
                                                          
MOVE d3
"d2-d3"
OK

MOVE Bb4
"Bf8-b4+"
OK

MOVE Nd2
"Nf3-d2"
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Coordinate Notation"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 16

//"Coordinate Notation"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"Coordinate notation is the easiest notation to learn.  This notation is also 
called Computer Notation."
OK

"To record a move in coordinate notation, write down the starting square of 
the move followed by the destination square."
OK

"Castling moves can be recorded using the algebraic method (O-O), or by using 
the starting and destination squares of the king.  For example, when white 
castles kingside, the move can be recorded as e1-g1 or O-O."
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
"Here's a sample game using coordinate notation:"
OK

MOVE e4
"e2-e4"
OK

MOVE e5
"e7-e5"
OK

MOVE Nf3
"g1-f3"
OK

MOVE Nc6
"b8-c6"
OK

MOVE Bb5
"f1-b5"
OK

MOVE a6
"a7-a6"
OK

MOVE Ba4
"b5-a4"
OK

MOVE Nf6
"g8-f6"
OK

MOVE Bc6
"4xc6 or a4-c6.  In coordinate notation, the 'x' for captures is optional."
OK
				
MOVE dxc6
"d7xc6"
OK

MOVE d3
"d2-d3"
OK

MOVE Bb4
"f8-b4+ or f8-b4.  In coordinate notation, the '+' for checks is optional."
OK

MOVE Nd2
"f3-d2"
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Descriptive Notation"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 17

//"Descriptive Notation"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"Although algebraic notation is now the preferred system of recording moves 
in virtually all chess magazines, tournaments and recent books, the 
descriptive system should be known by any serious chess enthusiast."
OK

"With few exceptions, chess literature in English and Spanish prior to the 
1980s is in descriptive notation."
OK

"As in algebraic notation, pieces are identified by letter:  K for King, 
Q for Queen, B for Bishop, and N for Knight.  Unlike algebraic, the Pawn 
is also identified by letter:  P."
OK

BOARD 56/R7 WHITE
"The squares are identified from the point of view of either White or Black.  
When White is moving, the rows are numbered from the bottom of the board to 
the top of the board with 1 through 8."
OK

MOVE Ra8
"The White Rook just moved from row 1 to row 8."
OK

BOARD r7/56 BLACK
"When Black is moving, the rows are numbered from the top of the board to 
the bottom."
OK

MOVE R8a1
"The Black rook (from Black's point of view) just moved from row 1 to row 8."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"The columns are named after the pieces that occupy the first row in the 
initial position."
OK

BOARD R7/R7/R7/R7/R7/R7/R7/R7 WHITE
"This column is called the Queen's Rook column, or QR."
OK

BOARD 1N6/1N6/1N6/1N6/1N6/1N6/1N6/1N6 WHITE
"This column is called the Queen's Knight column, or QN."
OK

BOARD 2B5/2B5/2B5/2B5/2B5/2B5/2B5/2B5 WHITE
"Queen's Bishop:  QB."
OK

BOARD 3Q4/3Q4/3Q4/3Q4/3Q4/3Q4/3Q4/3Q4 WHITE

"Queen:  Q."
OK

BOARD 4K3/4K3/4K3/4K3/4K3/4K3/4K3/4K3 WHITE
"King:  K."
OK

BOARD 5B2/5B2/5B2/5B2/5B2/5B2/5B2/5B2 WHITE
"King's Bishop:  KB."
OK

BOARD 6N1/6N1/6N1/6N1/6N1/6N1/6N1/6N1 WHITE
"King's Knight:  KN."
OK

BOARD 7R/7R/7R/7R/7R/7R/7R/7R WHITE
"And King's Rook:  KR."
OK

BOARD 32/1B6/24 WHITE
"In this example, the White Bishop is on Queen's Knight 4, or QN4."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"With descriptive notation, every attempt is made to avoid redundancy 
(some say clarity as well).  For example, if one Knight can move to KB4 
and the other Knight cannot move to either KB4 or QB4, then the move is 
recorded as N-B4, not N-KB4."
OK

BOARD 40/2N5/4N3/6N1 WHITE
GHOST WHITE KNIGHT e2
"If both Knights can move to the same square (K2 for example), the moves are 
distinguished by specifying which Knight is to make the move:  King's Knight 
or Queen's Knight: KN-K2 or QN-Q2."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"A capture is indicated by the abbreviation of the capturing piece, an x 
and the abbreviation of the captured piece.  Rook takes Bishop is recorded 
as RxB."
OK

"If more than one Rook can capture a Bishop, the capturing piece is further 
identified by King or Queen:  KRxB or QRxB."
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
"Here's a sample game using descriptive notation:"
OK

MOVE e4
"P-K4"
OK

MOVE e5
"P-K4 (from Black's point of view!)"
OK

MOVE Nf3
"N-KB3"
OK

MOVE Nc6
"N-QB3"
OK

MOVE Bb5
"B-N5 (only one Bishop can move to KN5 or QN5)."
OK

MOVE a6
"P-QR3"
OK

MOVE Bc6
"BxN (only one Bishop can capture a knight)."
OK

MOVE dxc6
"QPxB (QP instead of QNP)."
OK

MOVE d3
"P-Q3"
OK

MOVE Bb4
"B-N5ch (checks are indicated by ch)."
OK

MOVE Nc3
"N-B3"
OK

MOVE Nf6
"N-B3"
OK

MOVE O-O
"O-O"
OK

END CHAPTER

//==========================================================================
//"Correspondence Notation"
//==========================================================================

BEGIN CHAPTER 18
 //"Correspondence Notation"
BOARD 64 WHITE
//OK

"International correspondence notation is designed to avoid language problems 
(like different alphabets) when players from different countries compete by 
mail."
OK

"Each square is designated by a two digit number.  The first number replaces 
the file letter (a-h).  So, a is replaced by 1, b is 2, c is 3 and so on."
OK

"The second digit is the rank number, 1-8."
OK

BOARD 56/K7 WHITE
"In correspondence notation, the lower left square (a1) is 11..."
OK

BOARD K7/56 WHITE
"the upper left square (a8) is 18..."
OK

BOARD 7K/56 WHITE
"the upper right square (h8) is 88..."
OK

BOARD 56/7K WHITE
"and the lower right square (h1) is 81..."
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"There is no punctuation in correspondence notation.  e2-e4 is 5254."
OK

"Castling moves are recorded using the king starting and destination 
squares.  When white castles kingside (O-O), the move is 5171."
OK

BOARD rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/32/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR WHITE
"Here's a sample game using correspondence notation:"
OK

MOVE e4
"5254"
OK

MOVE e5
"5755"
OK

MOVE Nf3
"7163"
OK

MOVE Nc6
"2836"
OK

MOVE Bb5
"5125"
OK

MOVE a6
"1716"
OK

MOVE Ba4
"2514"
OK

MOVE Nf6
"7866"
OK

MOVE Bc6
"1436"
OK

MOVE dxc6
"4736"
OK

MOVE d3
"2223"
OK

MOVE Bb4
"6854"
OK

MOVE Nfd2
"6342"
OK

BOARD 64 WHITE
"For pawn promotions, the first two digits represent the starting square of 
the pawn.  The third digit is the destination file (1-8).  The new piece is 
represented by the fourth digit:  1 for Queen, 2 for Rook, 3 for Bishop and 
4 for Knight."
OK

BOARD n1r5/kP6/p1b5/q6p/5p2/6P1/1R3P1P/6K1 WHITE
"In this position, white's mating move..."
OK

"(b7xc8N++) is recorded in correspondence notation as 2734."
MOVE bxc8N
OK

